 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Polarity",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Pad 1 is the anode (+) and pad 2 the cathode (−). A green mark on one corner of the top face sits at the cathode end."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulsed drive",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Up to 60 mA is allowed in 0.1 ms pulses at a 1/10 duty cycle; continuous current is 30 mA at most, falling above 25 °C ambient along the maximum forward current curve to about 7 mA at 85 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Series resistor",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Drive at rated current with a current-limiting resistor in the circuit: a small change in voltage across the bare diode makes a large change in current that can destroy it. Colour shifts a little with drive current, and a resistor in series with each LED is recommended. Keep reverse voltage at 5 V or less when lighting or testing, and avoid surges at switch-on and switch-off."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Self-heating and ambient temperature both change the LED: as it warms, luminous efficiency drops and the colour moves, so heat removal belongs in the design. Relative intensity falls from about 1.11 at -40 °C to about 0.85 at 85 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Binning",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Parts are sorted by luminous intensity (P22 to P26), forward voltage (VM to VQ) and colour (DT1 to DT7). LEDs from different bins mixed in one product can show a visible colour difference."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The encapsulant is soft; pressure on the lens or body damages the emitting surface. Reflow along the profile no more than twice, using an easily washed flux and keeping the lens clean, since residue changes the colour. Hand soldering is for repair only: once, 300 °C at most, within 3 s, with an iron of 25 W or less. Keep tools off the lens while it is hot, and after soldering keep force and vibration off it and do not flex the board."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cleaning",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "No ultrasonic cleaning. Wipe or dip in isopropyl alcohol or pure alcohol for no longer than 1 minute, then let it stand 15 minutes at room temperature before use. Thinners, trichloroethylene, acetone, sulphides, nitrides, acids, alkalis and salts attack the part."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Potting and adhesives",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Volatile compounds can get into the package and, under light and heat in operation, discolour it and cause heavy light loss. Check every material that will touch the LED for compatibility, avoid adhesives that outgas organic vapour, and before potting keep a small trial batch lit at room temperature for 168 hours."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage and moisture",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The part is moisture sensitive. Sealed, store at 30 °C or less and below 60% RH and use within a year. Once opened, use within 7 days at 30 °C or less and 60% RH or less; moisture taken up can crack the encapsulant in reflow. Unused parts are baked before resealing in a foil bag or a nitrogen cabinet: reeled 60 ± 5 °C for 24 h, loose 105 ± 5 °C for 1 h. Keep away from acids, alkalis, corrosive gas, heavy vibration or a strong magnetic field."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Electrostatic discharge",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Static and voltage spikes damage the LED. Handle with grounded wrist strap, gloves, shoes and equipment. A damaged part shows higher leakage, a lower forward voltage at low current, or no light at low current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packing",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "4000 pieces to a reel of 8 mm carrier tape; the reel is labelled and packed in an anti-static, moisture-proof aluminium foil bag."
  }
 ],
